#import "statements.typ": *
#import "diagrams/47-k2.typ": *

== Milnor’s $K_2$-Group <sec:milnor-k2>

In the proof of Keating’s Theorem @th:keating-global-maximal-order-sk1 on
$S K_1$ of a maximal order, we have already seen that important information
about $S K_1$ of orders can be obtained by using Quillen’s Localization
Sequence. This sequence involves $K_2 lr((A))$, where $A$ is an algebra, and
indeed the full sequence involves all of the higher $K$-groups of Quillen.

The aim of this section is to give a brief introduction to $K_2$-groups,
following the treatment in Milnor @bib:Milnor1971. Other possible definitions of
$K_2$ were introduced by various authors in the past two decades, but it is
beyond the scope of this book to examine their interrelationships. As pointed
out by Milnor in @bib:Milnor1971, page viii, his version of $K_2$ is based on
the fundamental ideas of Steinberg @bib:Steinberg1962a, @bib:Steinberg1967.
Since it is convenient to have a name for these $K_2$-groups, and since the
terminology “Steinberg group” has another meaning (described below), we shall
therefore refer to the $K_2$-group defined in this section as _Milnor’s
$K_2$-group_. This usage agrees with the terminology in _Algebraic K-theory II_
(Bass @bib:Bass1973b), where Section C is titled “The Functor $K_2$ of Milnor.”

The authors wish to thank W. van der Kallen and M. Stein for many helpful
comments during the preparation of this section.

=== Steinberg Groups and $K_2$ <ss:steinberg-groups-k2>

Let $A$ be an arbitrary ring, and let $K_1 lr((A)) = GL lr((A)) slash E(A)$ as
in §@ss:elementary-matrices. Here,
$
  GL lr((A)) = limits(lim)_arrow.r GL_n lr((A)),
  quad E(A) = limits(lim)_arrow.r E_n lr((A)),
$
with $E_n lr((A))$ the subgroup of $GL_n lr((A))$ generated by all elementary
matrices $lr({bold(E)_(i j) lr((a))})$.
#source(201)
As we have seen, these matrices satisfy the Steinberg relations given in
@eq:steinberg-elementary-relations.

We now introduce the _Steinberg group_ $op("St")_n lr((A))$ for $n >= 3$, as the
abstract group generated by elements satisfying these relations. Explicitly, for
$n >= 3$, $op("St")_n lr((A))$ has generators
$ lr({x_(i j) lr((a)): 1 <= i,j <= n, i != j, a in A}), $
and relations
$
  cases(
    x_(i j) lr((a)) x_(i j) lr((b)) = x_(i j) lr((a+b)),
    [x_(i j) lr((a)),x_(k l) lr((b))] = 1
    quad "unless" j=k "or" i=l,
    [x_(i j) lr((a)),x_(j k) lr((b))] = x_(i k) lr((a b)),
  )
$
<eq:steinberg-group-relations>
for all $a,b in A$, and all choices of the indices between 1 and $n$.
_We agree once and for all that in expressions such as $x_(i j) lr((a))$,
$bold(E)_(i j) lr((a))$, etc., the subscripts are such that $i != j$._

The Steinberg group $op("St")_(n+1) lr((A))$ has more generators and more
relations than $op("St")_n lr((A))$, and there is a homomorphism
$op("St")_n lr((A)) arrow.r op("St")_(n+1) lr((A))$, defined by
$x_(i j) lr((a)) arrow.r x_(i j) lr((a))$, which need not be injective. Now
define
$
  "Steinberg group of" A = op("St") lr((A))
  = limits(lim)_arrow.r op("St")_n lr((A)),
$
#nidx("$op(\"St\") lr((A))$", "Steinberg group")
#idx("Steinberg group")
#idx("St(A)")
so $op("St") lr((A))$ has generators
$ lr({x_(i j) lr((a)): i >= 1,j >= 1,a in A}), $
and relations @eq:steinberg-group-relations.

There is a surjection
$
  phi: op("St") lr((A)) arrow.r E(A),
  quad "where" phi lr((x_(i j) lr((a)))) = bold(E)_(i j) lr((a)),
$
called the _evaluation map_. We put
$ "Milnor group of" A = K_2 lr((A)) = ker phi, $
#nidx("$K_2 lr((A))$", "Milnor group")
so there is an exact sequence of groups
$
  1 arrow.r K_2 lr((A)) arrow.r op("St") lr((A)) arrow.r^phi GL lr((A))
  arrow.r K_1 lr((A)) arrow.r 1.
$
<eq:milnor-k2-defining-sequence>
#idx("Milnor group", target: [@eq:milnor-k2-defining-sequence])
#idx("Evaluation map", target: [@eq:milnor-k2-defining-sequence])

Clearly $K_2$ is a functor from rings to groups, and each ring homomorphism
$f: A arrow.r A'$ induces a group homomorphism
$f_*: K_2 lr((A)) arrow.r K_2 lr((A'))$. The first important fact about $K_2$
is:

#proposition[
  The Milnor group $K_2 lr((A))$ is the center of $op("St") lr((A))$, and is
  therefore abelian.
] <prop:milnor-k2-steinberg-center>
#source(202)
#proof[
  _Step 1._ We show that $E(A)$ has trivial center. First, let $n >= 2$ and let
  $bold(z) = (z_(i j)) in "center of" E_n lr((A))$. Then
  $
    bold(z) dot bold(E)_(i j) lr((a)) = bold(E)_(i j) lr((a)) dot bold(z)
    quad "for all" i,j, quad 1 <= i,j <= n, quad a in A
  $
  (remember the convention that $i != j$). Comparing the $(i,j)$-entries of both
  sides, and then the $(i,i)$-entries, we find readily that
  $bold(z) = t bold(I)_n$ for some $t in "center of" A$.

  Now consider any element $xi in "center of" E(A)$; we may represent $xi$ by a
  matrix $bold(z) in E_(n-1) lr((A))$ for some $n$, and then
  $mat(bold(z), 0; 0, 1) in "center of" E_n lr((A))$. Therefore
  $mat(bold(z), 0; 0, 1) = t bold(I)_n$ for some $t$, which shows that $t = 1$
  and $bold(z) = bold(I)_(n-1)$, that is, $xi = 1$. We have thus proved that the
  center of $E(A)$ is $lr({1})$.

  _Step 2._ Now let $x in "center of" op("St") lr((A))$; since $phi$ maps
  $op("St") lr((A))$ onto $E(A)$, it follows that $phi(x) in "center of" E(A)$,
  and so $x in K_2 lr((A))$. Conversely, let $y in K_2 lr((A))$, so
  $phi(y) = 1$. We must show that $y$ commutes with each generator
  $x_(i j) lr((a))$ of $op("St") lr((A))$. Since $y in op("St") lr((A))$, we may
  express $y$ as a finite product
  $ y = product_m x_(i_m j_m) lr((a_m)), quad "where" a_m in A. $
  Choosing $n > op("Max")_m lr({i_m,j_m})$, surely $phi(y) in E_n lr((A))$.

  We now define
  $
    P_n = "subgroup of" op("St") lr((A))
    "generated by" lr({x_(i n) lr((a)): 1 <= i < n,a in A}),
  $
  <eq:steinberg-last-column-subgroup>
  and note that $P_n$ is abelian by virtue of @eq:steinberg-group-relations.
  Each $p in P_n$ is uniquely expressible as a product
  $p = product_(i=1)^(n-1) x_(i n) lr((a_i))$, and then
  $
    phi(p) = mat(
      delim: "[",
      1, 0, dots, 0, a_1;
      0, 1, dots, 0, a_2;
      dot, dot, dots, dot, dot;
      0, 0, dots, 0, 1;
    ).
  $
  Thus the evaluation map $phi$ is a monomorphism on $P_n$. The same holds for
  $Q_n$, where
  $
    Q_n = "subgroup of" op("St") lr((A))
    "generated by" lr({x_(n i) lr((a)): 1 <= i < n,a in A}).
  $
  Note that $Q_n$ is abelian by @eq:steinberg-group-relations.

  From the relations @eq:steinberg-group-relations, we have
  $
    x_(i j) lr((a)) dot P_n dot x_(i j) lr((a))^(-1) subset.eq P_n
    quad "for" 1 <= i,j <= n-1, quad a in A.
  $
  Now return to the given $y in K_2 lr((A))$; then $y P_n y^(-1) subset.eq P_n$,
  and we claim that $y$ centralizes $P_n$.
  #source(203)
  Indeed, since $phi(y) = 1$, we have
  $ phi lr((y p y^(-1))) = phi(p) quad "for all" p in P_n, $
  and therefore $y p y^(-1) = p$, since $phi$ is injective on $P_n$. Thus $y$
  centralizes $P_n$ (and also $Q_n$, by similar reasoning). But
  $ op("St")_n lr((A)) = ⟨P_n,Q_n⟩, quad n >= 3, $
  since
  $
    x_(i j) lr((a)) = [x_(i n) lr((a)),x_(n j) lr((1))]
    quad "for" 1 <= i,j < n, quad i != j, quad a in A.
  $
  Thus $y$ centralizes $op("St")_n lr((A))$, which completes the proof that
  $K_2 lr((A))$ is the center of $op("St") lr((A))$.
]

#remark[
  Let $U$ be the _upper triangular subgroup_ of $op("St") lr((A))$, generated by
  all
  $ lr({x_(i j) lr((a)): 1 <= i < j,a in A}). $
  The preceding proof shows that each $u in U$ is uniquely expressible as a
  finite product
  $
    u = x_(1 2) lr((a)) x_(1 3) lr((a')) dots x_(1 k) lr((a''))
    x_(2 3) lr((b)) x_(2 4) lr((b')) dots.
  $
  This implies at once that $phi$ is a monomorphism from $U$ into $E(A)$.
] <rem:steinberg-upper-triangular-injectivity>
#idx(
  "Upper triangular subgroup",
  target: [@rem:steinberg-upper-triangular-injectivity],
)

#definition[
  An exact sequence of groups
  $ 1 arrow.r C arrow.r E arrow.r^phi G arrow.r 1 $
  is a _central extension_ if $C <= "center of" E$. It is a _universal central
  extension_ if for each central extension
  $1 arrow.r C_1 arrow.r E_1 arrow.r G arrow.r 1$, there exists a unique
  homomorphism $E arrow.r E_1$ making the diagram
  #universal-central-extension-diagram
  commute. (Compare this concept with that of $B$-universal extensions defined
  in @i:def:universal-central-extension.) It is easily seen that if $G$ has a
  universal central extension, it must be unique up to isomorphism.
] <def:universal-central-group-extension>
#idx(
  "Universal central extension",
  target: [@def:universal-central-group-extension],
)

#source(204)
The following criterion for the existence of a universal central extension is
due originally to Schur, generalized later by H. Hopf; for a proof, see Milnor
@bib:Milnor1971, pages 43–46 or Kervaire @bib:Kervaire1970.

#theorem[
  A group $G$ has a universal central extension if and only if $G$ is _perfect_,
  that is, $G = [G,G]$.
] <th:universal-central-extension-perfect-criterion>

The proof of this theorem gives a method of constructing a universal central
extension of the group $G$, which we describe briefly. We consider any
presentation of $G$:
$ 1 arrow.r R arrow.r F arrow.r^phi G arrow.r 1, $
where $F$ is a free group, and $R$ the normal subgroup of relations. Let
$F' = [F,F]$. Then the exact sequence
$
  1 arrow.r (R inter F') slash [R,F] arrow.r F' slash [R,F]
  arrow.r^(phi') G arrow.r 1,
$
<eq:hopf-universal-central-extension>
where $phi'$ is induced by $phi$, gives a universal central extension of $G$.
Furthermore, we have
$ (R inter F') slash [R,F] tilde.equiv H_2 lr((G,ZZ)), $
the second homology group of $G$ with coefficients in $ZZ$. For a more detailed
discussion of this topic, see Rotman @bib:Rotman1979, Theorems 10.12 and 11.7,
or Gruenberg @bib:Gruenberg1970, Kervaire @bib:Kervaire1970, or Hilton–Stammbach
@bib:Hilton1971.

Now let $A$ be any ring; by @eq:steinberg-elementary-relations, the elementary
group $E(A)$ is perfect, and therefore has a universal central extension. The
next major result, due to Kervaire @bib:Kervaire1970 and Steinberg, gives the
connection between $K_2 lr((A))$ and this extension:

#theorem[
  The exact sequence
  $ 1 arrow.r K_2 lr((A)) arrow.r op("St") lr((A)) arrow.r^phi E(A) arrow.r 1 $
  is a universal central extension of $E(A)$. Consequently,
  $ K_2 lr((A)) tilde.equiv H_2 lr((E(A),ZZ)). $
  <eq:milnor-k2-second-homology>
] <th:steinberg-universal-central-extension>
#idx("Kervaire's Theorem", target: [@th:steinberg-universal-central-extension])

#remarks[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [For a proof, we refer the reader to Milnor @bib:Milnor1971 or Kervaire
      @bib:Kervaire1970.],
    [Let $F$ be a field, and put
      $
        SL lr((F)) = union.big_(n=1)^infinity SL_n lr((F))
        = lr({bold(X) in GL lr((F)): det bold(X) = 1}),
      $
      the _special linear group_ over $F$.
      #idx("Special linear group")
      #source(205)
      It follows readily from §@ss:elementary-matrices, or from the discussion
      of Dieudonné determinants in §@i:ss:reduced-norms, that $SL lr((F))$
      coincides with the elementary group $E(F)$. Formula
      @eq:milnor-k2-second-homology thus yields information about the homology
      group $H_2 lr((SL lr((F)),ZZ))$ in terms of $K_2 lr((F))$. The latter can
      be calculated explicitly by other methods; see the discussion in
      §@ss:k2-symbols.],
    [Let $n >= 3$, and let $A$ be any ring. We may define a group
      $K_2 lr((n,A))$ as the kernel of the surjection
      $phi: op("St")_n lr((A)) arrow.r E_n lr((A))$, so there is an exact
      sequence
      $
        1 arrow.r K_2 lr((n,A)) arrow.r op("St")_n lr((A))
        arrow.r^phi E_n lr((A)) arrow.r 1.
      $
      It turns out that when $A$ is a skewfield and $n >= 5$, this sequence
      gives a universal central extension of $E_n lr((A))$. On the other hand,
      for an arbitrary _commutative_ ring $A$, for $n >= 5$ the sequence again
      gives a universal central extension. This second result was proved by van
      der Kallen @bib:vanderKallen1977, using the previously known fact of
      “universality” (see Milnor @bib:Milnor1971, page 48).],
    [As an application of Theorem @th:steinberg-universal-central-extension, we
      shall show below that for any commutative ring $R$, the abelian group
      $K_2 lr((R))$ may be made into a $K_0 lr((R))$-module.],
    [The calculation of $K_2 lr((A))$ is by no means trivial even in the
      simplest cases. For example,
      $ K_2 lr((ZZ)) "is a cyclic group of order 2," $
      but the proof requires either a presentation of $GL_n lr((ZZ))$ by
      generators and relations, or else a topological argument. For details, see
      Milnor @bib:Milnor1971, §10.],
  )
] <rem:milnor-k2-variants>

To explain Remark (iv), we consider a commutative ring $R$. Since we may
identify the additive group $K_0 lr((R))$ with the Grothendieck ring
$G_0^R lr((R))$, it follows that $K_0 lr((R))$ is a commutative ring with
identity element $[R]$, and with multiplication defined by $times.o_R$. We have
already seen how to make $K_1 lr((R))$ into a $K_0 lr((R))$-module.
Specifically, for each $X in cal(P) lr((R))$ and each pair $[M,mu]$ with
$M in cal(P) lr((R))$ and $mu in Aut_R M$, we define
$ [X] [M,mu] = [X times.o_R M, 1 times.o mu] in K_1 lr((R)). $

Next, we may use formula @eq:milnor-k2-second-homology to define an action of
$K_0 lr((R))$ on $K_2 lr((R))$, so as to make the abelian group $K_2 lr((R))$
into a $K_0 lr((R))$-module. Given any $X in cal(P) lr((R))$, choose
$Y in cal(P) lr((R))$ so that $X plus.o Y tilde.equiv R^((k))$ for some $k$. Now
define a map
$ h_X: GL_n lr((R)) arrow.r GL_(k n) lr((R)) $
as follows: given $mu in GL_n lr((R))$, view it as element of $Aut R^((n))$, and
define $h_X lr((mu))$ by the formula
$
  h_X lr((mu)) lr({(x+y) times.o w}) = x times.o mu(w) + y times.o w,
  quad "for" x in X, quad y in Y, quad w in R^((n)).
$
#source(206)
Then $h_X lr((mu)) in Aut R^((k n)) tilde.equiv GL_(k n) lr((R))$. The map $h_X$
on $GL lr((R))$ is unique up to inner automorphisms, and satisfies
$ h_(X plus.o X') = h_X dot h_(X'), quad "for" X,X' in cal(P) lr((R)). $
It follows readily that $h_X$ depends only on the stable class of
$X in cal(P) lr((R))$, and that $h_X$ induces a homomorphism
$E_n lr((R)) arrow.r E(R)$ for $n >= 3$. There is then a well-defined map
$ (h_X)_*: H_2 lr((E_n lr((R)),ZZ)) arrow.r H_2 lr((E(R),ZZ)), $
additive in $X$. Since
$ H_2 lr((E(R),ZZ)) = limits(lim)_arrow.r H_2 lr((E_n lr((R)),ZZ)), $
we obtain a map $theta(X): H_2 lr((E(R),ZZ)) arrow.r H_2 lr((E(R),ZZ))$, which
makes $K_2 lr((R))$ into a $K_0 lr((R))$-module.

=== Relative $K$-Theory <ss:relative-k2>

We return next to the relative $K$-theory developed in §@sec:relative-k-theory,
so let $J$ be a two-sided ideal of the ring $A$, and let
$overline(A) = A slash J$. As shown in @cor:relative-k-theory-exact-sequence,
there is an exact sequence
$
  K_1 lr((A,J)) arrow.r K_1 lr((A)) arrow.r K_1 lr((overline(A)))
  arrow.r K_0 lr((A,J)) arrow.r K_0 lr((A)) arrow.r K_0 lr((overline(A))),
$
<eq:relative-k1-k0-sequence>
#idx("Relative K₀-group", target: [@eq:relative-k1-k0-sequence])
#idx("Relative K₁-group", target: [@eq:relative-k1-k0-sequence])
involving two relative groups $K_1 lr((A,J))$ and $K_0 lr((A,J))$. Our aim here
is to extend the sequence to the left by $K_2$ terms, and then to use this
longer sequence to also extend Milnor’s Mayer–Vietoris sequence
@eq:mayer-vietoris-sequence to the left.

Let $D$ be the double of $A$ along $J$, so as in
@eq:relative-double-fiber-product there is a fiber product
#numbered-block(relative-double-square)
<eq:k2-relative-double-fiber-product>
where the maps are ring surjections. As in
@eq:relative-k-groups-double-definition, we set
$ K_i lr((A,J)) = lr({y in K_i lr((D)): f(y) = 0}), quad i = 0,1. $
#source(207)
Then, as shown in the proof of @prop:relative-whitehead-double-isomorphism,
there is a commutative diagram with exact rows and columns:
#relative-whitehead-three-rows
with the $g$’s injective and the $f$’s surjective.

Now define the _relative Steinberg group_ $op("St") lr((A,J))$ as the kernel of
the map $op("St") lr((D)) arrow.r op("St") lr((A))$ induced by $f: D arrow.r A$,
so there is an exact sequence
$
  1 arrow.r op("St") lr((A,J)) arrow.r op("St") lr((D))
  arrow.r^f op("St") lr((A)) arrow.r 1.
$
#nidx("$op(\"St\") lr((A,J))$", "relative Steinberg group")
#idx("Relative Steinberg group")
#idx("St(A,I)")
#idx("Relative K-theory")

#lemma[
  There is an exact sequence
  $
    op("St") lr((A,J)) arrow.r^g op("St") lr((A))
    arrow.r^(f') op("St") lr((overline(A))) arrow.r 1.
  $
] <lem:relative-steinberg-quotient-exactness>
#proof[
  The map $g: D arrow.r A$ induces a map
  $op("St") lr((D)) arrow.r op("St") lr((A))$, and carries $op("St") lr((A,J))$
  into $op("St") lr((A))$. Further, $f'$ maps the generators of
  $op("St") lr((A))$ onto those of $op("St") lr((overline(A)))$, and $f' g = 1$.
  Thus, it remains to prove that $ker f' <= im g$ in the above sequence.

  To begin with, each generator of $op("St") lr((D))$ is of the form
  $x_(i j) lr((a,b))$ with $(a,b) in D$, that is, $a equiv b (mod J)$. We may
  write
  $
    x_(i j) lr((a,b)) = x_(i j) lr((a,a)) x_(i j) lr((0,b-a))
    = (Delta_* x_(i j) lr((a))) x_(i j) lr((0,c)),
  $
  where $Delta: A arrow.r D$ is the diagonal map, and where $c = b-a in J$. Now
  let $w in op("St") lr((A,J))$, so $f(w) = 1$ in $op("St") lr((A))$. We may
  write
  $
    w = product_m (Delta_* x_(i_m j_m) lr((a_m)))
    x_(i_m j_m) lr((0,c_m)),
  $
  with each $c_m in J$. Then
  $ 1 = f(w) = product x_(i_m j_m) lr((a_m)) = product s_m, $
  with $s_m = x_(i_m j_m) lr((a_m)) in op("St") lr((A))$. Therefore
  $product Delta_* s_m = 1$, and $w$ is expressible as a product of terms of the
  form
  $
    (Delta_* s) x_(i j) lr((0,c)) dot Delta_* lr((s^(-1))),
    quad "with" s in op("St") lr((A)), quad c in J.
  $
  <eq:relative-steinberg-normal-generators>
  #source(208)
  Therefore $g lr((op("St") lr((A,J))))$ is generated by all expressions
  $ lr({s x_(i j) lr((c)) s^(-1): s in op("St") lr((A)),c in J}), $
  so $im(g)$ is the normal closure in $op("St") lr((A))$ of the subgroup
  generated by $lr({x_(i j) lr((c)): c in J})$. It follows that $op("cok")(g)$
  is obtained from $op("St") lr((A))$ by imposing on the generators
  $lr({x_(i j) lr((a)): a in A})$ of $op("St") lr((A))$ the additional relations
  $x_(i j) lr((c)) = 1$ for $c in J$. Therefore $op("cok")(g)$ is identical with
  the Steinberg group $op("St") lr((overline(A)))$, which completes the proof.
]

#corollary[
  The composite map
  $ op("St") lr((A,J)) arrow.r^g op("St") lr((A)) arrow.r^phi E(A) $
  gives a surjection
  $ op("St") lr((A,J)) arrow.r E(A,J). $
] <cor:relative-steinberg-elementary-surjection>
#proof[
  We have seen that $g lr((op("St") lr((A,J))))$ is generated by all expressions
  $ lr({s x_(i j) lr((c)) s^(-1): s in op("St") lr((A)),c in J}). $
  Since $phi lr((op("St") lr((A)))) = E(A)$, it follows that $im phi g$ is the
  normal subgroup of $E(A)$ generated by all
  $lr({bold(E)_(i j) lr((c)): c in J})$. This is precisely $E(A,J)$, by
  @eq:relative-elementary-subgroup.
]

#definition[
  The _relative $K_2$-group_ $K_2 lr((A,J))$ is the kernel of the surjection
  $op("St") lr((A,J)) arrow.r E(A,J)$ given above.
] <def:relative-milnor-k2-group>
#idx("Relative K₂-group", target: [@def:relative-milnor-k2-group])

We thus obtain a commutative diagram with exact rows and columns:
#relative-k2-three-rows
with upper vertical arrows injective, lower ones surjective. Combining this
diagram with the preceding one, we obtain a new diagram with the same
properties:
#relative-full-k2-three-rows

#source(209)
Applying the Snake Lemma to the diagram
#relative-steinberg-snake-diagram
we obtain an exact sequence
$
  K_2 lr((A,J)) arrow.r K_2 lr((A)) arrow.r K_2 lr((overline(A)))
  arrow.r K_1 lr((A,J)) arrow.r K_1 lr((A)) arrow.r K_1 lr((overline(A))).
$
<eq:relative-milnor-k2-k1-sequence>
This sequence extends to the left the sequence given in
@eq:relative-k1-k0-sequence. Once we prove the Excision Lemma
@lem:relative-whitehead-excision for the relative group $K_1 lr((A,J))$, it will
be a simple task to obtain Milnor’s extended Mayer–Vietoris sequence (Theorem
@th:milnor-extended-mayer-vietoris).

Before going further, however, we should point out that the relative groups
$op("St") lr((A,J))$ and $K_2 lr((A,J))$, as defined above, are not the
“correct” ones, from a more advanced point of view. They are “too large” to
allow the sequence @eq:relative-milnor-k2-k1-sequence to be extended further to
the left, as shown by Swan @bib:Swan1971. The correct relative groups were given
independently by Keune @bib:Keune1978 and Loday @bib:Loday1978, and we now
summarize some of their results.

Given any ring homomorphism $theta: A arrow.r overline(A)$, it follows from
Quillen’s work that there is a long exact sequence of higher $K$-groups:
$
  dots arrow.r K_3 lr((A)) arrow.r K_3 lr((overline(A)))
  arrow.r K_2 lr((theta)) arrow.r K_2 lr((A)) arrow.r K_2 lr((overline(A)))
  arrow.r K_1 lr((theta)) arrow.r K_1 lr((A)) arrow.r dots,
$
<eq:quillen-relative-higher-k-sequence>
where the $K_i lr((theta))$ are relative $K$-groups. In particular, suppose that
$ theta: A arrow.r A slash J = overline(A) $
<eq:relative-k-theory-quotient-map>
is the natural surjection, where $J$ is a two-sided ideal of the ring $A$. We
have already defined relative groups $K_i lr((A,J))$ for $i = 0,1,2$ (see
@rem:swan-relative-grothendieck-group, @eq:relative-whitehead-group, and
@def:relative-milnor-k2-group). It turns out that $K_i lr((A,J))$ coincides with
$K_i lr((theta))$ for $i = 0,1$, but not when $i = 2$.

We shall now define the “correct” relative group $K_2 lr((theta))$, following
the approach of Keune and Loday. We begin by introducing a new _relative
Steinberg group_ $op("St") lr((theta))$, where $theta$ is as in
@eq:relative-k-theory-quotient-map. Let $D$ be the double of $A$ along $J$, as
in @eq:k2-relative-double-fiber-product. Changing notation for the moment, let
$f_*: op("St") lr((D)) arrow.r op("St") lr((A))$ be the surjection of Steinberg
groups induced by the ring surjection $f: D arrow.r A$; likewise, let $g$ induce
$g_*: op("St") lr((D)) arrow.r op("St") lr((A))$. We had previously defined
$op("St") lr((A,J))$ to be $ker f_*$, and we now set#footnote[
  If $H_1$ and $H_2$ are subgroups of a group $G$, then $[H_1,H_2]$ denotes the
  subgroup of $G$ generated by all commutators $[h_1,h_2]$ with $h_i in H_i$.
]
$ op("St") lr((theta)) = (ker f_*) slash [ker f_*,ker g_*], $
#idx("Relative Steinberg group")
#idx("Relative K-theory")
a factor group of $op("St") lr((A,J))$.
#source(210)
The commutator group $[ker f_*,ker g_*]$ lies in the kernel of the map
$op("St") lr((A,J)) arrow.r E(A,J)$, that is, in $K_2 lr((A,J))$. We now define
$ K_2 lr((theta)) = K_2 lr((A,J)) slash [ker f_*,ker g_*]. $
With this choice for $K_2 lr((theta))$, Keune and Loday proved that the sequence
@eq:quillen-relative-higher-k-sequence is exact, starting on the left with the
term $K_3 lr((A))$. For further discussion, we refer the reader to their
articles.

We are now ready to return to our original task of extending the sequence
@eq:relative-k1-k0-sequence to the left, using the relative $K_2 lr((A,J))$ as
defined in @def:relative-milnor-k2-group. We begin by proving the following
“excision lemma” for relative $K_1$-groups, analogous to the $K_0$-excision
theorem in @th:relative-grothendieck-excision:

#lemma(name: [Excision Lemma])[
  Let $psi: A arrow.r A'$ be a surjection of rings, and let $J$ be a two-sided
  ideal of $A$ such that $J inter ker psi = 0$. Set $J' = psi(J)$, a two-sided
  ideal of $A'$. Then $psi$ induces a surjection
  $ K_2 lr((A,J)) arrow.r K_2 lr((A',J')) $
  and an isomorphism
  $ K_1 lr((A,J)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((A',J')). $
] <lem:relative-whitehead-excision>
#idx("Excision Theorem", target: [@lem:relative-whitehead-excision])
#proof[
  Clearly $J'$ is a two-sided ideal of $A'$, since $psi(A) = A'$. We show now
  that $psi$ induces an isomorphism
  $ psi: GL lr((A,J)) tilde.equiv GL lr((A',J')). $
  Indeed, if $bold(X) in GL_n lr((A,J))$ is such that
  $psi lr((bold(X))) = bold(I)_n$, then $psi lr((bold(X)-bold(I))) = 0$, and
  therefore $bold(X)-bold(I) = 0$ since $psi$ is injective on $J$. To show $psi$
  surjective, let $bold(U) in GL_n lr((A',J'))$ and set
  $bold(V) = bold(U)^(-1)$. Since $psi(J) = J'$, we can find matrices $bold(X)$
  and $bold(Y)$ with entries in $J$, such that
  $
    psi lr((bold(X))) = bold(U)-bold(I),
    quad psi lr((bold(Y))) = bold(V)-bold(I).
  $
  Then $(bold(X)+bold(I))(bold(Y)+bold(I))-bold(I)$ has entries in $J$, and is
  annihilated by $psi$, hence must be 0. It follows that
  $bold(X)+bold(I) in GL_n lr((A,J))$ and $psi lr((bold(X)+bold(I))) = bold(U)$.
  Thus $psi$ is surjective, and we have established the desired isomorphism.

  Next, the proof of @lem:relative-steinberg-quotient-exactness shows that
  $op("St") lr((A',J'))$ is generated by expressions like those in
  @eq:relative-steinberg-normal-generators, so $psi$ induces a surjection
  $alpha: op("St") lr((A,J)) arrow.r op("St") lr((A',J'))$. The lemma now
  follows by diagram-chasing, starting with the commutative diagram with exact
  rows:
  #relative-excision-comparison
  where $alpha$ is surjective and $psi$ is an isomorphism.
]

#source(211)
#corollary[
  Keeping the hypotheses of @lem:relative-whitehead-excision, set
  $overline(A) = A slash J$, $overline(A)' = A' slash J'$. Then there is an
  exact sequence of groups
  $
    K_2 lr((A)) arrow.r K_2 lr((A')) times K_2 lr((overline(A)))
    arrow.r K_2 lr((overline(A)')) arrow.r K_1 lr((A))
    arrow.r K_1 lr((A')) times K_1 lr((overline(A)))
    arrow.r K_1 lr((overline(A)')).
  $
] <cor:excision-absolute-k2-k1-sequence>
#idx("Mayer-Vietoris sequence", target: [@cor:excision-absolute-k2-k1-sequence])
#proof[
  Comparing the relative sequences for the pairs $(A,J)$ and $(A',J')$, we
  obtain a commutative diagram with exact rows:
  #excision-relative-sequences-comparison
  Note that $gamma_1$ is surjective and $gamma_4$ is an isomorphism, by
  @lem:relative-whitehead-excision.

  Now consider the sequence
  $
    K_2 lr((A)) arrow.r^((gamma_2,alpha_2))
    K_2 lr((A')) times K_2 lr((overline(A)))
    arrow.r^((beta_2,1 slash gamma_3)) K_2 lr((overline(A)'))
    arrow.r^(alpha_4 gamma_4^(-1) beta_3)
  $
  $
    K_1 lr((A)) arrow.r^((gamma_5,alpha_5))
    K_1 lr((A')) times K_1 lr((overline(A)))
    arrow.r^((beta_5,1 slash gamma_6)) K_1 lr((overline(A)')),
  $
  where, by definition,
  $
    (gamma_2,alpha_2): x arrow.r (gamma_2 x,alpha_2 x),
    quad (beta_2,1 slash gamma_3): (y,z) arrow.r beta_2 y slash gamma_3 z,
  $
  and so on. It is easily checked that the composite of any two consecutive maps
  is 0.

  Next, we verify that
  $ker(beta_2, 1 slash gamma_3) subset.eq im(gamma_2, alpha_2)$. Let
  $(y,z) in ker(beta_2, 1 slash gamma_3)$, so $beta_2 y = gamma_3 z$. Then
  $beta_3 gamma_3 z = 0$, so $alpha_3 z = 0$ because $gamma_4$ is injective.
  Therefore $z = alpha_2 x$ for some $x in K_2 lr((A))$. This gives
  $ beta_2 y = gamma_3 alpha_2 x = beta_2 gamma_2 x, $
  so $beta_2 lr((y^(-1) dot gamma_2 x)) = 0$. Since $gamma_1$ is surjective, we
  obtain $y = gamma_2 lr({x dot alpha_1(w)})$ for some $w in K_2 lr((A,J))$.
  This gives
  $
    (y,z) = (gamma_2,alpha_2) lr({x dot alpha_1(w)})
    in im(gamma_2, alpha_2),
  $
  as desired. We leave the remaining verifications to the reader.
]

We are now ready to establish:

#source(212)
#theorem(name: [Milnor’s Theorem])[
  Given a fiber product of rings
  #milnor-fiber-product-square
  in which both $g_1$ and $g_2$ are surjective, there is an exact Mayer–Vietoris
  sequence of groups:
  $
    K_2 lr((A)) arrow.r K_2 lr((A_1)) times K_2 lr((A_2))
    arrow.r K_2 lr((overline(A))) arrow.r^partial K_1 lr((A))
    arrow.r K_1 lr((A_1)) times K_1 lr((A_2)) arrow.r K_1 lr((overline(A)))
  $
  $
    arrow.r^partial K_0 lr((A)) arrow.r K_0 lr((A_1)) plus.o K_0 lr((A_2))
    arrow.r K_0 lr((overline(A))).
  $
  The maps are given by
  $
    K_i lr((A)) arrow.r^((f_1,f_2)) K_i lr((A_1)) times K_i lr((A_2)),
    quad K_i lr((A_1)) times K_i lr((A_2))
    arrow.r^((g_1,1 slash g_2)) K_i lr((overline(A))),
  $
  for $i = 1,2$, while for $i = 0$ these become
  $
    K_0 lr((A)) arrow.r^((f_1,f_2)) K_0 lr((A_1)) plus.o K_0 lr((A_2)),
    quad K_0 lr((A_1)) plus.o K_0 lr((A_2))
    arrow.r^((g_1,-g_2)) K_0 lr((overline(A))).
  $
  The connecting homomorphisms $partial$ are defined as in
  @cor:excision-absolute-k2-k1-sequence, and the sequence extends the
  Mayer–Vietoris sequence @eq:mayer-vietoris-sequence.
] <th:milnor-extended-mayer-vietoris>
#proof[
  We remark first that since $g_1$ and $g_2$ are surjective, so are $f_1$ and
  $f_2$. Thus we may write
  $ A_1 = A slash J, quad overline(A) = A_2 slash J', $
  for some two-sided ideals $J$ and $J'$ of $A$ and $A_2$, respectively. From
  the fiber product, we find readily that $f_2$ induces an isomorphism
  $ker f_1 tilde.equiv ker g_2$, that is, $J tilde.equiv J'$. The desired result
  now follows from @cor:excision-absolute-k2-k1-sequence and
  @eq:mayer-vietoris-sequence, using the ring surjection $A arrow.r A_2$.
]

As an example of the use of the above sequence, consider the integral group ring
$ZZ G$ of a cyclic group $G$ of prime order $p$. The fiber product in
@exm:rim-cyclic-prime-projective-class-group gives an exact sequence
$
  K_2 lr((overline(ZZ))) arrow.r K_1 lr((ZZ G)) arrow.r^i
  K_1 lr((ZZ)) times K_1 lr((R)) arrow.r^h K_1 lr((overline(ZZ))),
$
in which $h$ is surjective.
#source(213)
We shall see in §@ss:k2-symbols that $K_2 lr((F)) = 0$ for any finite field $F$.
Thus, the map $i$ is injective. Furthermore, $K_1 lr((ZZ)) tilde.equiv ZZ^dot$
and $K_1 lr((R)) tilde.equiv R^dot$, the latter by virtue of the
Bass–Milnor–Serre Theorem. It follows at once that
$ K_1 lr((ZZ G)) tilde.equiv (ZZ G)^dot quad "and" S K_1 lr((ZZ G)) = 1. $
<eq:cyclic-prime-integral-group-ring-sk1-trivial>
As we shall see in §@sec:sk1-integral-group-rings,
@eq:cyclic-prime-integral-group-ring-sk1-trivial is true for an arbitrary finite
cyclic group $G$.

=== Symbols <ss:k2-symbols>

Throughout the discussion, $A$ denotes an arbitrary ring, and $R$ a commutative
ring. One technique for calculating $K_2 lr((A))$ or $K_2 lr((R))$, in special
cases, is to introduce certain types of generators of these groups, satisfying
some obvious relations. These special generators are called _symbols_#idx(
  "Symbols",
), by analogy with the Legendre and Jacobi symbols of number theory, and in fact
this similarity is by no means accidental.

For the case of a field $F$, the _Steinberg symbols_#idx("Steinberg symbols")
$lr({u,v})$, defined for $u,v in F^dot$, will generate $K_2 lr((F))$, and will
satisfy some simple relations. If instead we consider a commutative local ring
$R$, then $K_2 lr((R))$ will be generated by the _Dennis–Stein symbols_#idx(
  "Dennis-Stein symbols",
) $⟨a,b⟩$, defined for $a,b in R$ with $1-a b in R^dot$; these symbols will
satisfy a different set of defining relations.

In this survey, we shall follow closely the treatment in Milnor @bib:Milnor1971
and the two articles by Dennis and Stein in Bass @bib:Bass1973b, omitting most
of the proofs. Our aim is to give the reader a feeling for the flavor of the
subject, and also to provide some insight into the homomorphism
$ theta': K_2 lr((L)) arrow.r product.co_Q K_1 lr((S slash Q)) $
occurring in the proof of Keating’s Theorem
@th:keating-global-maximal-order-sk1. Here, $L$ is a global field which is the
quotient field of a Dedekind domain $S$, and $Q$ ranges over all maximal ideals
of $S$.

To emphasize the importance of the Milnor group $K_2$, we shall present here
Tate’s calculation of $K_2 lr((QQ))$, which has as consequences the Hilbert
Reciprocity Theorem and the Law of Quadratic Reciprocity!

We recall from §@ss:steinberg-groups-k2 that for $n >= 3$, the _Steinberg group_
$op("St")_n lr((A))$ has generators $lr({x_alpha lr((a)): a in A})$, where
$alpha$ ranges over all ordered pairs $i j$ with $1 <= i,j <= n$, $i != j$, and
where these generators satisfy the Steinberg relations
@eq:steinberg-group-relations. For $alpha = i j$, we define $-alpha = j i$, the
reversed pair (compare with Exercise @exc:type-a-root-system).

Let $phi: op("St")_n lr((A)) arrow.r E_n lr((A))$ be the homomorphism given by
the evaluation map, which carries each generator $x_alpha lr((a))$ onto the
elementary matrix $bold(E)_alpha lr((a))$. Let $K_2 lr((n,A)) = ker phi$, so
there is an exact sequence of groups
$
  1 arrow.r K_2 lr((n,A)) arrow.r op("St")_n lr((A))
  arrow.r^phi E_n lr((A)) arrow.r 1.
$
<eq:finite-rank-steinberg-extension>

We now introduce certain combinations of generators of $op("St")_n lr((A))$,
whose images under the map $phi$ are especially useful types of matrices.
#source(214)
Let $u in A^dot$, and set
$
  w_alpha lr((u)) = x_alpha lr((u)) x_(-alpha) lr((-u^(-1))) x_alpha lr((u)),
  quad "and" h_alpha lr((u)) = w_alpha lr((u)) w_alpha lr((-1)),
$
<eq:steinberg-weyl-torus-elements>
for each $alpha = i j$ as above. For $alpha = 1 2$, we find readily that
$
  w_alpha lr((u)) arrow.r^phi mat(0, u; -u^(-1), 0) plus.o bold(I)^((n-2)),
  quad h_alpha lr((u)) arrow.r^phi mat(u, 0; 0, u^(-1)) plus.o bold(I)^((n-2)),
$
<eq:steinberg-weyl-evaluation>
where $bold(I)$ denotes an identity matrix of appropriate size.

Likewise, if $a,b$ are elements of a commutative ring $R$ and $1-a b in R^dot$,
then for each $alpha$ we set
$
  H_alpha lr((a,b)) = x_(-alpha) lr((-b(1-a b)^(-1)))
  x_alpha lr((-a)) x_(-alpha) lr((b)) x_alpha lr(((1-a b)^(-1) a))
  in op("St")_n lr((R)),
$
<eq:dennis-stein-lift-element>
and we find readily that
$
  H_alpha lr((a,b)) arrow.r^phi op("diag") lr((1-a b,(1-a b)^(-1)))
  plus.o bold(I)^((n-2)) in E_n lr((R)).
$

Our next step is to introduce the _Weyl group_ $W_n$, defined as the subgroup of
$op("St")_n lr((A))$ generated by all elements
$lr({w_alpha lr((u)): u in A^dot})$, where (as above) $alpha = i j$, with
$1 <= i != j <= n$, and where $n >= 3$. From @eq:steinberg-weyl-evaluation, we
find readily that $phi lr((W_n))$ consists precisely of all generalized
permutation matrices, whose entries are in $A^dot$, and whose determinant equals
1 (in $A^dot slash [A^dot,A^dot]$); see Milnor @bib:Milnor1971, §9 for details.
Now set
$ C_n = lr({w in W_n: phi(w) = 1}), $
<eq:weyl-evaluation-kernel>
#idx("Weyl subgroup of Stₙ(A)", target: [@eq:weyl-evaluation-kernel])
a subgroup of $W_n$ and of $op("St")_n lr((A))$. Further, with $n >= 3$ as
usual, it turns out that $C_n$ lies in the center of $op("St")_n lr((A))$; see
Milnor @bib:Milnor1971, §9.

Let us now restrict our attention to the case of a commutative ring $R$, and let
$n >= 3$. For each $alpha = i j$ and each $u in R^dot$, we have
$h_alpha lr((u)) in W_n$. From @eq:steinberg-weyl-evaluation it follows at once
that for each $alpha$,
$
  lr({u,v}) = h_alpha lr((u v)) h_alpha lr((u))^(-1) h_alpha lr((v))^(-1)
  in C_n, quad "for" u,v in R^dot,
$
<eq:steinberg-symbol>
thereby defining elements $lr({u,v}) in C_n subset.eq K_2 lr((n,R))$. It turns
out that $lr({u,v})$ is independent of the choice of $alpha$. We call these
elements $lr({u,v})$ _Steinberg symbols_; they satisfy the following identities:
$
  lr({u u',v}) = lr({u,v}) lr({u',v}),
  quad lr({u,v}) = lr({v,u})^(-1), quad lr({u,-u}) = 1,
$
<eq:steinberg-symbol-basic-identities>
for $u,u',v in R^dot$. Furthermore,
$ lr({u,1-u}) = 1 quad "if both" u in R^dot "and" 1-u in R^dot. $
<eq:steinberg-symbol-unit-complement>
(see Milnor @bib:Milnor1971, §§8–9 for proofs of the above assertions.)

#source(215)
#remarks[
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [The term “symbol” is also used to denote a map $lr({quad,quad})$ from
      $R^dot times R^dot$ into some abelian group, such that the identities
      @eq:steinberg-symbol-basic-identities and
      @eq:steinberg-symbol-unit-complement are valid. Thus, the map
      $ (u,v) in R^dot times R^dot arrow.r lr({u,v}) in C_n, $
      with $C_n$ as in @eq:weyl-evaluation-kernel, yields a symbol on $R$.],
    [As shown in Milnor @bib:Milnor1971, Theorem 9.11, the group $C_n$ is
      generated by the set of all symbols $lr({u,v})$ with $u,v in R^dot$. On
      the other hand, if $R$ is either a finite field or a residue class ring
      $ZZ slash p^n ZZ$, with $p$ an odd prime, it is easily shown that all
      symbols on $R$ must be trivial (Exercise
      @exc:finite-ring-symbols-trivial).],
  )
] <rem:symbol-definitions-triviality>

The importance of the Steinberg symbols $lr({u,v}) in C_n$ is that they provide
concrete elements of $K_2 lr((n,R))$, for $R$ commutative. For the case of
fields and skewfields, even stronger results are known:

#theorem[
  Let $A$ be a skewfield, and define $K_2 lr((n,A))$ as in
  @eq:finite-rank-steinberg-extension. Then
  #enum(
    numbering: "(i)",
    [For $n >= 3$,
      $ K_2 lr((n,A)) = C_n <= "center of" op("St")_n lr((A)), $
      so $K_2 lr((n,A))$ is an abelian group.],
    [For $n >= 5$, the exact sequence @eq:finite-rank-steinberg-extension gives
      a universal central extension of $E_n lr((A))$.],
  )
] <th:skewfield-finite-rank-k2-center>

For a proof of (i), see Milnor @bib:Milnor1971, Theorem 9.12. Assertion (ii)
follows from the proof of @th:steinberg-universal-central-extension; see Milnor
@bib:Milnor1971, Theorem 5.10 and also Remark @rem:milnor-k2-variants(iii)
above.

#corollary[
  Let $F$ be a field. Then $K_2 lr((F))$ is generated by the set of all
  Steinberg symbols $lr({u,v})$, with $u,v in F^dot$. Further, $K_2 lr((F)) = 1$
  if $F$ is a finite field.
] <cor:field-k2-symbol-generation>
#proof[
  We have
  $ K_2 lr((F)) = limits(lim)_arrow.r K_2 lr((n,F)), $
  and $K_2 lr((n,F)) = C_n$ for $n >= 3$. Now use the fact that $C_n$ is
  generated by Steinberg symbols (see @rem:symbol-definitions-triviality(ii)).
  Finally, $K_2 lr((F)) = 1$ since symbols on a finite field are trivial.
]

A much deeper result, fundamental to the entire theory, is as follows:

#theorem(name: [Matsumoto’s Theorem])[
  Let $F$ be a field. Then the abelian group $K_2 lr((F))$ has a presentation
  with generators $lr({u,v})$ with $u,v in F^dot$, subject to the relations
  $
    lr({u u',v}) = lr({u,v}) lr({u',v}),
    quad lr({u,v v'}) = lr({u,v}) lr({u,v'}),
  $
  and also#footnote[
    Any bimultiplicative map $f: F^dot times F^dot arrow.r G$
    ($= "abelian multiplicative group"$), such that $f lr((u,1-u)) = 1$ for
    $u != 0,1$, is necessarily a symbol on $F$. See Exercise
    @exc:bimultiplicative-steinberg-relation-symbol.
  ]
  $ lr({u,1-u}) = 1 quad "for" u != 0,1. $
  #source(216)
  Furthermore, with $C_n$ as in @eq:weyl-evaluation-kernel,
  $ C_3 tilde.equiv C_4 tilde.equiv dots tilde.equiv K_2 lr((F)). $
] <th:matsumoto-k2-field>
#idx("Matsumoto's Theorem", target: [@th:matsumoto-k2-field])

A proof is given in Milnor @bib:Milnor1971, §12, and a simpler proof in Keune
@bib:Keune1975.

Matsumoto’s Theorem can be generalized in two ways, one in which $F$ is replaced
by a skewfield $D$, and the other with $F$ replaced by a commutative local ring
$R$. For the skewfield case, we have (Rehmann @bib:Rehmann1978):

#theorem(name: [Rehmann’s Theorem])[
  Let $D$ be a skewfield, and let $op("St")_1 lr((D))$ be the group generated by
  elements $c(u,v)$, one for each pair of elements $u,v in D^dot$, and subject
  to the defining relations
  $ c(u v,w) = c(u v u^(-1),u w u^(-1)) c(u,w), $
  $ c(u,v w) c(v,w u) c(w,u v) = 1, quad c(u,1-u) = 1, $
  if $u,v,w in D^dot$ (and $u != 1$ in the last relation). Then there is an
  exact sequence
  $
    1 arrow.r K_2 lr((D)) arrow.r op("St")_1 lr((D))
    arrow.r^f [D^dot,D^dot] arrow.r 1,
  $
  where $f: c(u,v) arrow.r [u,v]$ for $u,v in D^dot$.

  Indeed, there is an injection $op("St")_1 lr((D)) arrow.r op("St") lr((D))$,
  given by
  $ c(u,v) arrow.r lr({u,v}) quad "for" u,v in D^dot, $
  where $lr({u,v})$ is defined as in @eq:steinberg-symbol, using $alpha = 1 2$.
  This identifies $ker f$ with $K_2 lr((D))$.
] <th:rehmann-skewfield-k2-presentation>
#idx("Rehmann's Theorem", target: [@th:rehmann-skewfield-k2-presentation])
#idx("Steinberg symbols", target: [@th:rehmann-skewfield-k2-presentation])

We turn next to the _Dennis–Stein symbols_ $⟨a,b⟩$, where $a$ and $b$ are
elements of a commutative ring $R$, and are such that $1-a b in R^dot$. Let
$alpha = i j$, where $1 <= i != j <= n$, and where $n >= 3$. We define
$ ⟨a,b⟩ = H_alpha lr((a,b)) h_alpha lr((1-a b))^(-1), $
<eq:dennis-stein-symbol>
#idx("Dennis-Stein symbols", target: [@eq:dennis-stein-symbol])
#idx("Symbols", target: [@eq:dennis-stein-symbol])
with $H_alpha$ and $h_alpha$ as in @eq:dennis-stein-lift-element and
@eq:steinberg-weyl-torus-elements, respectively. It turns out that $⟨a,b⟩$ lies
in $K_2 lr((n,R))$, and does not depend on the choice of $alpha$. Dennis and
Stein originally used $⟨-a,b⟩$ in place of our $⟨a,b⟩$; their original list of
relations was considerably simplified by Maazen–Stienstra.
#source(217)
Later, Silvester and Stienstra independently observed that the new symbols
satisfy identities of a more symmetric form, as compared to the original
relations.

In the following discussion, we shall only use the Dennis–Stein symbol $⟨a,b⟩$
when $1-a b in R^dot$; likewise, when we use the Steinberg symbol $lr({u,v})$,
we assume automatically that $u,v in R^dot$. The Dennis–Stein symbols satisfy
the following identities in $K_2 lr((n,R))$:
$
  cases(
    ⟨a,b⟩ ⟨b,a⟩ = 1,
    ⟨a,b⟩ ⟨a,c⟩ = ⟨a,b+c-a b c⟩,
    ⟨a,b c⟩ = ⟨a b,c⟩ ⟨a c,b⟩,
  )
$
<eq:dennis-stein-symbol-identities>

One can express Steinberg symbols $lr({u,v})$ in terms of Dennis–Stein symbols
$⟨a,b⟩$ (but not conversely, in certain examples!).

Dennis and Stein proved (see Dennis–Stein @bib:DennisStein1973b, §2):

#theorem[
  Let $n >= 3$ and let $R$ be a commutative semilocal ring. Then $K_2 lr((n,R))$
  and $K_2 lr((R))$ are generated by the set of all Dennis–Stein symbols. They
  are also generated by the set of all Steinberg symbols.
] <th:semilocal-k2-symbol-generation>

It is in terms of Dennis–Stein symbols that one obtains a presentation of the
group $K_2 lr((R))$, for $R$ a commutative local ring. This result provides a
second generalization of Matsumoto’s Theorem (which is used in the proof, in any
case) and is based on the work of Dennis–Stein, Maazen–Stienstra, and van der
Kallen (see van der Kallen @bib:vanderKallen1980). We have

#theorem[
  Let $R$ be a commutative local ring. Then $K_2 lr((R))$ has a presentation
  with generators all Dennis–Stein symbols $⟨a,b⟩$, one for each pair $a,b in R$
  such that $1-a b in R^dot$, and with relations
  @eq:dennis-stein-symbol-identities whenever all the symbols involved are
  defined.
] <th:local-k2-dennis-stein-presentation>

We now return to Matsumoto’s Theorem and show how to use it to obtain Tate’s
formula for $K_2 lr((QQ))$.

Matsumoto’s Theorem implies at once that for any field $F$, the Steinberg symbol
$ lr({quad,quad}): F^dot times F^dot arrow.r K_2 lr((F)), $
defined in @eq:steinberg-symbol, is universal. Thus (see Exercise
@exc:bimultiplicative-steinberg-relation-symbol) every bimultiplicative map $f$
from $F^dot times F^dot$ into an abelian group $G$, such that
$f lr((u,1-u)) = 1$ for $u != 0,1$, factors uniquely through $K_2 lr((F))$. This
means that there exists a unique homomorphism $h: K_2 lr((F)) arrow.r G$ such
that
$ h lr({x,y}) = f(x,y) quad "for all" x,y in F^dot. $
Choosing $f$ suitably, we may thus construct well-defined homomorphisms on
$K_2 lr((F))$.
#source(218)
This idea lies at the heart of Tate’s calculation of $K_2 lr((QQ))$, which we
now give.

#theorem(name: [Tate’s Theorem])[
  There is an isomorphism
  $
    K_2 lr((QQ)) tilde.equiv lr({plus.minus 1}) plus.o
    product.co_(p >= 3) op("GF") lr((p))^dot,
  $
  the direct sum of a cyclic group of order 2 and cyclic groups of order $p-1$,
  with $p$ ranging over all odd primes.
] <th:tate-k2-rationals>
#idx("Tate's Theorem", target: [@th:tate-k2-rationals])
#proof[
  _Step 1._ We shall give the isomorphism explicitly in terms of certain symbols
  defined on $QQ$. First, let
  $ t_infinity: RR^dot times RR^dot arrow.r lr({plus.minus 1}) $
  be the bimultiplicative map defined by
  $
    t_infinity lr((a,b)) = cases(
      1 quad & "if" a > 0 "or" b > 0,
      -1 quad & "if" a < 0 "and" b < 0.
    )
  $
  Since $t_infinity lr((a,1-a)) = 1$ for $a != 0,1$, there is a well-defined
  homomorphism
  $
    t_infinity: K_2 lr((RR)) arrow.r lr({plus.minus 1}),
    quad "given by" lr({a,b}) arrow.r t_infinity lr((a,b))
    "for" a,b in RR^dot.
  $
  Since $t_infinity lr({-1,-1}) = -1$, we conclude that $lr({-1,-1}) != 1$ in
  $K_2 lr((RR))$; this inequality also holds in $K_2 lr((QQ))$ and
  $K_2 lr((ZZ))$, since $t_infinity$ is also a symbol on every subring of $RR$.

  _Step 2._ For each rational odd prime $p$, we introduce a _tame symbol_
  $ t_p: QQ^dot times QQ^dot arrow.r op("GF") lr((p))^dot, $
  as follows. Each $x in QQ^dot$ can be expressed as $x = p^e a slash b$, where
  $a,b in ZZ$ and $p divides.not a b$. Put $v(x) = e$, and call $v$ the
  _exponential $p$-adic valuation_ on $QQ$; let $v(0) = infinity$. Since
  $ v lr((x^(v(y)) slash y^(v(x)))) = 0, $
  the quotient has a well-defined image
  $overline(x^(v(y)) slash y^(v(x))) in op("GF") lr((p))^dot$. Setting
  $
    t_p lr((x,y)) = (-1)^(v(x) v(y)) overline(x^(v(y)) slash y^(v(x)))
    quad "for" x,y in QQ^dot,
  $
  we find readily that for each $p$, $t_p$ is a symbol on $QQ$. Thus there is a
  well-defined homomorphism
  $
    t_p: K_2 lr((QQ)) arrow.r op("GF") lr((p))^dot,
    quad "given by" lr({a,b}) arrow.r t_p lr((a,b))
    "for" a,b in QQ^dot.
  $
  #idx("Tame symbol")
  #idx("Exponential valuation")

  #source(219)
  We intend to prove that the map
  $
    lr({a,b}) in K_2 lr((QQ)) arrow.r
    t_infinity lr((a,b)) + sum_(p >= 3) t_p lr((a,b)),
    quad "for" a,b in QQ^dot,
  $
  gives the desired isomorphism.

  _Step 3._ For $m >= 1$, let $S_m$ be the subgroup of $K_2 lr((QQ))$ generated
  by all symbols $lr({a,b})$ with $a,b in ZZ$, $abs(a) <= m$, $abs(b) <= m$.
  Then
  $
    K_2 lr((QQ)) = union.big_(m=1)^infinity S_m
    = limits(lim)_arrow.r S_m.
  $
  Now $S_1$ is cyclic of order 2, generated by $lr({-1,-1})$. Also $S_2 = S_1$,
  since
  $
    lr({-1,2}) = lr({u,1-u}) = 1 quad ("with" u = -1),
    quad lr({2,2}) = lr({-1,2}) lr({-2,2}) = 1.
  $
  Thus $t_infinity: S_2 tilde.equiv lr({plus.minus 1})$, with $t_infinity$ as in
  Step 1.

  We now show that for $p >= 3$, the map $t_p$ induces an isomorphism
  $ t'_p: S_p slash S_(p-1) tilde.equiv op("GF") lr((p))^dot. $
  <eq:tate-symbol-filtration-quotient>
  Note that $t'_p$ is well defined since $t_p$ is trivial on $S_(p-1)$. Further,
  if $a in ZZ$ is prime to $p$, then $v(a) = 0$, $v(p) = 1$, so
  $ t_p lr({a,p}) = overline(a) in op("GF") lr((p))^dot. $
  Thus $t'_p$ is surjective, so it remains for us to construct an inverse map
  $ theta: op("GF") lr((p))^dot arrow.r S_p slash S_(p-1). $
  <eq:tate-symbol-quotient-inverse>
  Let $a in ZZ$, $0 < a < p$, and put
  $ theta lr((overline(a))) = lr({a,p}) quad (mod S_(p-1)), $
  viewed as element of the multiplicative group $S_p slash S_(p-1)$. We shall
  now verify that
  $
    theta lr((overline(a))) theta lr((overline(b))) = theta lr((overline(a b)))
    quad "for" 0 < a,b < p.
  $
  Write
  $ a b = r p + c, quad "where" 0 <= r <= p-1, 0 < c < p. $
  If $r = 0$, then
  $ theta lr((overline(a b))) = lr({c,p}) = lr({a,p}) lr({b,p}) $
  as desired.
  #source(220)
  On the other hand, let $r > 0$; then
  $
    lr({(r p)/(a b),c/(a b)}) = 1
    quad "because" (r p)/(a b) + c/(a b) = 1.
  $
  Consequently we obtain
  $
    lr({c,(r p)/(a b)}) = lr({a b,(r p)/(a b)})
    = lr({a b,r/(a b)}) lr({a b,p})
    equiv lr({a,p}) lr({b,p}) quad (mod S_(p-1)),
  $
  where the congruence is interpreted multiplicatively. But also
  $
    lr({c,(r p)/(a b)}) = lr({c,p}) lr({c,r/(a b)})
    equiv lr({c,p}) quad (mod S_(p-1)),
  $
  which completes the proof that $theta$ is a well-defined homomorphism. Clearly
  $t'_p theta$ is the identity map, and it remains to check that $theta$ is
  surjective. Now $S_p slash S_(p-1)$ has generators
  $lr({plus.minus p,plus.minus p})$ and $lr({plus.minus b,plus.minus p})$, where
  $0 < b <= p-1$. However,
  $ lr({p,-p}) = 1 = lr({-p,p}), quad lr({1-p,p}) = 1, $
  $
    lr({p,p}) = lr({-1,p}) lr({-p,p}) = lr({-1,p})
    equiv lr({p-1,p}) = theta lr((overline(p-1)))^(-1).
  $
  Also, for $0 < b <= p-1$,
  $ lr({-b,p}) = lr({b,p}) lr({-1,p}) in im theta. $
  Thus $theta$ is surjective, and is the inverse of $t'_p$, which proves
  @eq:tate-symbol-filtration-quotient.

  _Step 4._ We are now ready to complete the proof, and begin by computing $S_m$
  explicitly. If $m >= 4$ is not prime, then of course $S_m = S_(m-1)$. We have
  already shown that $t_infinity: S_2 tilde.equiv lr({plus.minus 1})$. We now
  use induction on the prime $p$ to show that
  $
    tau_p: S_p tilde.equiv lr({plus.minus 1}) times op("GF") lr((3))^dot
    times dots times op("GF") lr((p))^dot,
    quad "where" tau_p = t_infinity times t_3 times dots times t_p.
  $
  <eq:tate-finite-symbol-decomposition>
  The results holds at $p = 2$, so let $p >= 3$ and assume the result true at
  all smaller primes. Let $q$ be the largest prime $< p$, so $S_(p-1) = S_q$.
  Then there is a commutative diagram with exact rows
  #tate-symbol-filtration-comparison
  #source(221)
  Since $tau_q$ is an isomorphism by the induction hypothesis, so is $tau_p$ by
  the Snake Lemma. This proves @eq:tate-finite-symbol-decomposition for all $p$.
  However, we then obtain
  $
    K_2 lr((QQ)) = limits(lim)_arrow.r S_m = lr({plus.minus 1}) times
    product.co_(p >= 3) op("GF") lr((p))^dot,
  $
  and the proof of Tate’s Theorem is completed.
]

Let us now sketch the connection between $K_2 lr((QQ))$ and quadratic
reciprocity. For $p$ an odd prime, define the _Hilbert symbol_ $(a,b)_p$ by the
formula
$
  (a,b)_p = t_p lr((a,b))^((p-1) slash 2) in op("GF") lr((p))^dot
  quad "for" a,b in QQ^dot.
$
<eq:odd-prime-hilbert-symbol>
#idx("Hilbert symbol", target: [@eq:odd-prime-hilbert-symbol])
Then $(a,b)_p$ is bimultiplicative, and is unchanged if $a$ is replaced by
$a x^2$ with $x in QQ^dot$. Further, for $a,b in ZZ$ prime to $p$, we find that
$
  (a,b)_p = 1, quad (a,p)_p = overline(a)^((p-1) slash 2)
  = cases(
    1 quad & "if" overline(a) = "square in" op("GF") lr((p)),
    -1 quad & "otherwise."
  )
$
Furthermore,
$ (p,p)_p = (-1)^((p-1) slash 2). $
Thus, if $a in ZZ$ is prime to $p$, then $(a,p)_p$ is the usual Legendre symbol
$lr((a/p))$.

We now define $(a,b)_2 = plus.minus 1$, for $a,b in QQ^dot$, according to
whether the equation
$ a x^2 + b y^2 = z^2, quad "with" x,y,z in QQ_2 quad (= "2-adic field"), $
has a nontrivial solution or not (see Exercises @exc:odd-prime-hilbert-conic and
@exc:two-adic-hilbert-symbol). Then $(a,b)_2$ defines a symbol on $QQ^dot$, the
_Hilbert symbol_ associated with the prime 2.

Since every symbol on $QQ$ factors through $K_2 lr((QQ))$ (according to the
discussion preceding @th:tate-k2-rationals), it follows from Tate’s formula
@th:tate-k2-rationals that there exist homomorphisms
$
  phi_infinity: lr({plus.minus 1}) arrow.r lr({plus.minus 1}),
  quad phi_p: op("GF") lr((p))^dot arrow.r lr({plus.minus 1}), quad p >= 3,
$
such that
$
  (a,b)_2 = phi_infinity t_infinity lr((a,b)) dot
  product_(p >= 3) phi_p t_p lr((a,b)) quad "for all" a,b in QQ^dot.
$
Since the values of $phi_p$ lie in $lr({plus.minus 1})$, we have
$phi_p lr((x^2)) = 1$ for all $x in op("GF") lr((p))^dot$, and therefore
$ phi_p lr((x)) = x^(n_p (p-1) slash 2), quad x in op("GF") lr((p))^dot, $
with $n_p = 0$ or 1.
#source(222)
Thus we obtain
$
  (a,b)_2 = t_infinity lr((a,b))^(n_infinity) dot
  product_(p >= 3) (a,b)_p^(n_p) quad "for all" a,b in QQ^dot,
$
where also $n_infinity = 0$ or 1. Choosing $a = b = -1$, we see that
$n_infinity = 1$.

It is convenient to write
$ t_infinity lr((a,b)) = (a,b)_infinity, $
so now
$ (a,b)_2 = (a,b)_infinity dot product_(p >= 3) (a,b)_p^(n_p). $
<eq:hilbert-symbol-two-factorization>
We show that each $n_p = 1$. First, choose $b = p = 8 k plus.minus 3$, $a = 2$;
then
$
  (2,p)_2 = -1, quad (2,p)_infinity = 1,
  quad (2,p)_q = 1 quad "for" q = "odd prime," quad q != p.
$
This gives
$ -1 = (2,p)_p^(n_p), quad "whence" n_p = 1 quad "and" (2,p)_p = -1. $
Second, choose $b = p = 8 k + 7$, $a = -1$, and then we get as above:
$ (-1,p)_p = -1 quad "and" n_p = 1. $
Finally we need (see Milnor @bib:Milnor1971, Lemma 11.9):

#lemma[
  Let $p = 8 k + 1$ be prime. Then there exists an odd prime $q < sqrt(p)$ such
  that $p$ is a quadratic nonresidue mod $q$, that is, $(p,q)_q = -1$.
] <lem:small-quadratic-nonresidue-prime>

Continuing with our discussion, suppose $n_p != 1$ for some odd prime $p$, and
let $p$ be minimal such. Choose $q$ as in the lemma, so $n_q = 1$. Then
$
  (p,q)_infinity = 1, quad (p,q)_2 = 1,
  quad (p,q)_l = 1 quad "for" l = "odd prime," quad l != p,q.
$
Therefore @eq:hilbert-symbol-two-factorization gives
$ 1 = (p,q)_q^(n_q) (p,q)_p^(n_p) = -1 dot (p,q)_p^(n_p), $
so $n_p = 1$, a contradiction. We have thus proved:

#theorem(name: [Hilbert Reciprocity Theorem])[
  For $a,b in QQ^dot$,
  $ product_(2 <= p <= infinity) (a,b)_p = 1. $
] <th:hilbert-reciprocity>
#idx("Hilbert Reciprocity Theorem", target: [@th:hilbert-reciprocity])

#source(223)
#corollary(title: [Law of Quadratic Reciprocity])[
  Let $p$ and $q$ be odd primes, and let $lr((x/p))$ denote the Legendre symbol.
  Then
  $
    lr((-1/p)) = (-1)^((p-1) slash 2),
    quad lr((2/p)) = (-1)^((p^2-1) slash 8),
  $
  and
  $ lr((p/q)) lr((q/p)) = (-1)^((p-1)(q-1) slash 4). $
] <cor:quadratic-reciprocity>
#idx("Quadratic Reciprocity Theorem", target: [@cor:quadratic-reciprocity])
#proof[
  We have
  $
    (-1,p)_infinity = 1, quad (-1,p)_2 = (-1)^((p-1) slash 2),
    quad (-1,p)_l = 1,
  $
  for $l$ an odd prime, $l != p$. By @th:hilbert-reciprocity we obtain
  $ lr((-1/p)) = (-1,p)_p = (-1)^((p-1) slash 2). $
  A similar argument yields the formula for $lr((2/p))$.

  Finally, we have
  $
    (p,q)_infinity = 1, quad (p,q)_l = 1
    quad "for odd primes" l != p,q,
  $
  $
    (p,q)_p = lr((q/p)), quad (p,q)_q = lr((p/q)),
    quad (p,q)_2 = (-1)^((p-1)(q-1) slash 4).
  $
  Combining these results, we obtain the desired formula for the product
  $lr((p/q)) lr((q/p))$, which completes the proof.
]

Suppose now that $R$ is any Dedekind domain, and let $F$ be its quotient field.
As shown by Quillen (see Dennis–Stein @bib:DennisStein1973a, page 246), there is
an exact sequence
$
  K_3 lr((F)) arrow.r product.co_P K_2 lr((R slash P))
  arrow.r K_2 lr((R)) arrow.r K_2 lr((F))
  arrow.r product.co_P K_1 lr((R slash P)) arrow.r K_1 lr((R))
$
$
  arrow.r K_1 lr((F)) arrow.r product.co_P K_0 lr((R slash P))
  arrow.r K_0 lr((R)) arrow.r K_0 lr((F)) arrow.r 0,
$
where $P$ ranges over all (nonzero) prime ideals of $R$.

Now assume that $F$ is a global field; then each $R slash P$ is a finite field,
so $K_2 lr((R slash P)) = 1$ by @cor:field-k2-symbol-generation. Further, the
map $K_1 lr((R)) arrow.r K_1 lr((F))$ is injective, since the kernel
$S K_1 lr((R))$ is trivial by the Bass–Milnor–Serre Theorem. #source(224) The
above sequence thus breaks into two exact sequences
$
  0 arrow.r K_2 lr((R)) arrow.r K_2 lr((F))
  arrow.r^d product.co_P K_1 lr((R slash P)) arrow.r 0,
$
$
  1 arrow.r K_1 lr((R)) arrow.r K_1 lr((F))
  arrow.r product.co_P K_0 lr((R slash P))
  arrow.r K_0 lr((R)) arrow.r K_0 lr((F)) arrow.r 0.
$
The second of these is our standard localization sequence
@cor:algebra-whitehead-localization. The map $d$ played a vital role in the
proof of Keating’s Theorem @th:keating-global-maximal-order-sk1, and it is
therefore of interest to give an explicit description of the map in the special
case considered here. It will suffice to define the map
$ d_P: K_2 lr((F)) arrow.r (R slash P)^dot tilde.equiv K_1 lr((R slash P)) $
for each $P$; it will be clear from its definition that for each
$xi in K_2 lr((F))$, $d_P lr((xi)) = 1$ a.e.

Now $K_2 lr((F))$ is generated by Steinberg symbols $lr({x,y})$ with
$x,y in F^dot$. We define $d_P$ by constructing a bimultiplicative map
$d_P: F^dot times F^dot arrow.r (R slash P)^dot$, such that
$d_P lr((x,1-x)) = 1$ for $x != 0,1$, and then factoring this map through
$K_2 lr((F))$. Let $v_P$ be the _exponential $P$-adic valuation_ on $F$, defined
analogously to the valuation $v$ occurring in Step 2 of the proof of Tate’s
Theorem (see also §@i:ss:valuations-completions). We now set
$
  d_P lr({x,y}) = (-1)^(v_P lr((x)) v_P lr((y)))
  overline(x^(v_P lr((y))) slash y^(v_P lr((x)))) in (R slash P)^dot
  quad "for" x,y in F^dot,
$
the right-hand side representing a _tame symbol_ corresponding to $P$. (The
reader will easily verify that this is indeed a symbol on $F$.)
#idx("Tame symbol")
#idx("Exponential valuation")
We can now describe the map $d$ in terms of these $lr({d_P})$, and we have
$ d: K_2 lr((F)) arrow.r product.co_P K_1 lr((R slash P)), $
where for $x,y in F^dot$,
$ d lr({x,y}) = - product.co_P d_P lr({x,y}). $
(The minus sign is used for technical reasons, which we shall not discuss here.)

#exercises-heading

#exercise[
  Let $R$ be a commutative ring, and $K_0 lr((R)) = G_0^R lr((R))$ its
  Grothendieck ring, with multiplication given by $times.o_R$. For each ideal
  $J$ of $R$, show that the relative groups $K_0 lr((R,J))$ and $K_1 lr((R,J))$
  can be made into $K_0 lr((R))$-modules.
] <exc:relative-k-groups-projective-module-action>

#exercise[
  Let $F$ be a noetherian ring of finite global dimension, and let $x,y$ be
  indeterminates which commute with all elements of $F$ and with each other. Let
  $A = F[x,y] slash (x y)$.
  #source(225)
  Show that there is a fiber product
  #polynomial-axis-fiber-product
  and apply @eq:relative-milnor-k2-k1-sequence to prove that
  $K_1 lr((A)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((F))$.
  #hint([@exc:polynomial-axes-whitehead-homotopy])[
    Since there is a map $F arrow.r F[x]$ which splits the surjection
    $F[x] arrow.r F[x] slash (x) = F$, the maps
    $K_i lr((F[x])) arrow.r K_i lr((F))$, $i = 1,2$, are surjective.
  ]
] <exc:polynomial-axes-whitehead-homotopy>

#exercise[
  Let $p$ be an odd prime, and let $(a,b)_p$ be the Hilbert symbol defined by
  @eq:odd-prime-hilbert-symbol. Let $QQ_p$ be the $p$-adic field. Prove that
  $(a,b)_p = 1$ if the equation
  $ a x^2 + b y^2 = 1 quad "has a solution with" x,y in QQ_p, $
  and $(a,b)_p = -1$ otherwise. Show also that $(a,b)_p = 1$ if and only if
  $a x^2 + b y^2 = z^2$ has a nontrivial solution with $x,y,z in QQ_p$.
] <exc:odd-prime-hilbert-conic>

#exercise[
  Define $(a,b)_2$ analogously, for $a,b in ZZ$, $a b != 0$. Prove that if
  $2 divides.not a b$, then
  $ (a,b)_2 = (-1)^((a-1)(b-1) slash 4). $
  Show also that
  $ (a,2)_2 = (-1)^((a^2-1) slash 8), quad (2,2)_2 = 1. $
  Prove that the map
  $
    QQ^dot times QQ^dot arrow.r lr({plus.minus 1}),
    quad "given by" x times y arrow.r (x,y)_2,
  $
  defines a symbol on $QQ$.
] <exc:two-adic-hilbert-symbol>

#exercise[
  Prove the following version of the *Excision Lemma
  @lem:relative-whitehead-excision*:

  _Let $B = B_1 times dots times B_n$ be a product of rings, and $A$ a subring
  of $B$ which projects onto each $B_i$. Let $J$ be a two-sided ideal of $B$
  contained in $A$. Show that $K_1 lr((A,J)) tilde.equiv K_1 lr((B,J))$, and
  that the map $K_2 lr((A,J)) arrow.r K_2 lr((B,J))$ is surjective._
  #hint([@exc:subdirect-product-relative-excision])[
    See Bass @bib:Bass1968, Theorem 5.8 on page 484, and Dennis
    @bib:Dennis1973b, pages 117–119.
  ]
] <exc:subdirect-product-relative-excision>

#exercise[
  Let $R = op("GF") lr((q))$ or $ZZ slash p^n ZZ$, with $p$ odd. Show that all
  symbols on $R$ are trivial.
  #hint([@exc:finite-ring-symbols-trivial])[
    Consider the case $R = op("GF") lr((q))$, $q$ odd. There must exist two
    squares $a,b in R^dot$ whose sum is a nonsquare, since otherwise the set of
    squares is a subfield of $R$. Therefore there exist nonsquares
    $c,d in R^dot$ with $c+d = 1$. If $lr({quad,quad})$ is a symbol on $R$, then
    $lr({c,d}) = 1$. Now let $R^dot = ⟨x⟩$, so $c$ and $d$ are odd powers of
    $x$. Then $lr({x,x})$ has odd order. But $lr({x,x}) = lr({x,x})^(-1)$, so
    $lr({x,x}) = 1$, and therefore $lr({u,v}) = 1$ for all $u,v in R^dot$. If
    $q$ is even, the order of $lr({x,x})$ divides both 2 and the odd number
    $q-1$, so $lr({x,x}) = 1$. Analogous arguments work where
    $R = ZZ slash p^n ZZ$, $p$ odd.
  ]
] <exc:finite-ring-symbols-trivial>

#exercise[
  Let $F$ be a field, $G$ an abelian multiplicative group, and let
  $f: F^dot times F^dot arrow.r G$ be any bimultiplicative map such that
  $f lr((u,1-u)) = 1$ for $u != 0,1$. Prove that $f$ is a symbol on $F$.
  #source(226)
  #hint([@exc:bimultiplicative-steinberg-relation-symbol])[
    We must show that for $u,v in F^dot$,
    $ f(u,-u) = 1 quad "and" f(v,u) = f(u,v)^(-1). $
    For the first of these, note that $-u = (1-u) slash (1-u^(-1))$ if $u != 1$.
    Therefore
    $
      f(u,-u) = f(u,1-u) f(u,1-u^(-1))^(-1)
      = f(u,1-u^(-1))^(-1) = f(u^(-1),1-u^(-1)) = 1.
    $
    For the second identity, use
    $ f(u,v) = f(u,v) f(u,-u) f(u v,-u v)^(-1) f(v^(-1),-v^(-1)) $
    $
      = f(v^(-1),-u v) f(v^(-1),-v^(-1))
      = f(v^(-1),u) = f(v,u)^(-1).
    $
  ]
] <exc:bimultiplicative-steinberg-relation-symbol>

#exercise[
  Let $A$ be a finite $p$-torsion ring, where $p$ is prime. Prove that
  $K_2 lr((A))$ is a finite $p$-group.
  #hint([@exc:finite-p-ring-milnor-k2], caption: [Hint (Oliver):])[
    Since $A$ is semilocal, it has stable range 1. The analogue of
    @th:surjective-whitehead-stability for $K_2$ shows that $K_2 lr((A))$ is
    f.g., so it suffices to prove that $K_2 lr((A))$ is a $p$-torsion group. For
    any group $G$, let $H_2 lr((G))$ denote its Schur multiplier
    $H_2 lr((G,ZZ))$. Given $N ⊴ G$, let $overline(G) = G slash N$. Then (see
    Rotman @bib:Rotman1979, page 356) $H_2 lr((G))$ has a filtration whose
    factor groups are subquotients of the groups #named-equation(
      name: [$(ast)$],
    )[
      $
        H_2 lr((overline(G))), quad H_1 lr((overline(G),H_1 lr((N)))),
        quad H_0 lr((overline(G),H_2 lr((N)))).
      $
    ] <eq:p-torsion-homology-factors>
    We apply this to the case where $G = E(A)$, $overline(G) = E(overline(A))$,
    with $overline(A) = A slash rad A$, a direct sum of full matrix algebras
    over finite fields. Then
    $H_2 lr((overline(G))) = K_2 lr((overline(A))) = 1$, by
    @cor:field-k2-symbol-generation. On the other hand, $N$ is the kernel of the
    surjection $E(A) arrow.r E(overline(A))$, hence is the union of kernels of
    $E_n lr((A)) arrow.r E_n lr((overline(A)))$ for $n >= 2$. Each of these
    kernels lies in $1+(rad A) M_n lr((A))$ and is therefore a finite $p$-group.
    Thus $N = limits(lim)_arrow.r N_i$, with each $N_i$ a finite $p$-group.
    Therefore $H_1 lr((N)) = limits(lim)_arrow.r H_1 lr((N_i))$ is a $p$-torsion
    group, and likewise so is $H_2 lr((N))$. Thus the groups
    @eq:p-torsion-homology-factors are $p$-torsion, whence so is
    $H_2 lr((E(A)))$ ($= K_2 lr((A))$).
  ]
] <exc:finite-p-ring-milnor-k2>
